<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Baruch Spinoza</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1632">1632</a>–<a href="/years/1677">1677</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Benedictus de Spinoza, Benedict de Spinoza</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Baruch Spinoza was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> philosopher from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>'s Portuguese Jewish community, which expelled him in 1656. His Theological-Political Treatise of 1670 argued for freedom of thought and a historical reading of the Bible, and his Ethics, published after his death, set out a philosophy identifying God with nature. He is counted among the founders of modern rationalism and of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/age-of-enlightenment">Enlightenment</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/baruch-spinoza.jpg" alt="Baruch Spinoza" width="320" height="371" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spinoza.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
